WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.744
Post-quantum cryptography prepares systems for a future where certain current public-key algorithms

00:00:04.744 --> 00:00:07.066
may no longer provide adequate protection.

00:00:07.066 --> 00:00:10.549
It does not require defenders to run quantum computers,

00:00:10.549 --> 00:00:14.418
and it is not the same as quantum key distribution.

00:00:14.418 --> 00:00:19.836
It is a standards-aware migration effort for ordinary systems that depend on key establishment,

00:00:19.836 --> 00:00:22.932
digital signatures, certificates, software signing, remote access, identity,

00:00:22.932 --> 00:00:24.093
and trusted communication.

00:00:24.093 --> 00:00:27.963
The most important planning move is to understand cryptographic roles.

00:00:27.963 --> 00:00:31.059
A key encapsulation mechanism helps establish shared secrets.

00:00:31.059 --> 00:00:33.768
A digital signature supports authentication and integrity.

00:00:33.768 --> 00:00:38.024
Symmetric encryption and hashing have different risk profiles than public-key mechanisms.

00:00:38.024 --> 00:00:39.572
Once roles are clear,

00:00:39.572 --> 00:00:43.829
teams can build an inventory that records where cryptography is used,

00:00:43.829 --> 00:00:48.086
what depends on it, what data it protects, who owns it,

00:00:48.086 --> 00:00:50.021
and how migration-ready it is.

00:00:50.021 --> 00:00:54.665
A strong migration program prioritizes risk, uses vetted standards and mature libraries,

00:00:54.665 --> 00:00:58.148
tests interoperability, coordinates with vendors, and builds crypto agility.

00:00:58.148 --> 00:01:02.791
It avoids custom cryptography and avoids unsupported combinations that cannot be maintained.

00:01:02.791 --> 00:01:07.048
It also recognizes that engineering details matter: size, performance, certificates, protocols,

00:01:07.048 --> 00:01:11.692
hardware, logging, monitoring, and operational support can all shape the migration path.

00:01:11.692 --> 00:01:16.723
The goal is not to predict the exact date of a quantum threat.

00:01:16.723 --> 00:01:21.754
The goal is to reduce future migration risk before urgent replacement becomes necessary.

00:01:21.754 --> 00:01:24.463
Teams that start with inventory, governance, pilots,

00:01:24.463 --> 00:01:29.880
and practical communication will be better prepared than teams that wait for a deadline.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:35.298
PQC is ultimately a resilience project: know what you depend on, make change possible,

00:01:35.298 --> 00:01:37.620
and move deliberately toward standards-based protection.
